<h2>India's second-largest spice export, led by China</h2>
<p>Cumin is India's second-largest spice export by value after dried red chilli, and the destination pattern echoes chilli closely: China leads by a clear margin. Calendar 2024 figures show China importing USD 140.7 million of Indian cumin, more than 50% ahead of the second-place market, Bangladesh (USD 91.7 million), out of USD 784.9 million in total Indian cumin exports worldwide across roughly 237,241 MT.</p>

<h2>Why China leads this trade</h2>
<p>As with chilli, the driver is China's scale of food-processing and culinary demand combined with India's production capacity and reliability of supply. Cumin is a staple ingredient across Chinese cuisine in specific regional traditions and in broader food manufacturing, and India's export infrastructure — established cleaning, sorting and export-documentation capacity — makes it a natural fit for buyers needing consistent large-volume supply.</p>

<h2>Specification checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — whole seed or ground; whole retains aroma longer and is the more common export form</li>
  <li><strong>Purity</strong> — freedom from admixture, other seeds and foreign matter</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — affects storage stability and shelf life</li>
  <li><strong>Volatile oil content</strong> — the aroma-intensity parameter that matters most to food manufacturers</li>
</ul>
<p>Specify these clearly and we'll quote against them rather than a generic "export quality" tier.</p>

<h2>GACC registration applies here too</h2>
<p>Cumin falls under the food categories requiring facility-level registration with China Customs (GACC) under Decree 248, the same framework covering red chilli. If you haven't confirmed registration status with your supplier, that's the first question to resolve — it has real lead time and can't be sorted during a shipment's transit.</p>

<h2>Beyond China</h2>
<p>Bangladesh, the UAE and the United States round out India's top cumin markets in the data we've verified, each at meaningfully smaller scale than China but still significant volumes in their own right. If China isn't your specific market, see our broader <a href="/products/spices/">spices category page</a> for the full picture across destinations.</p>

<h2>Sourcing regions and harvest timing</h2>
<p>Cumin is grown predominantly in Gujarat and Rajasthan, with harvest running February through April. Fresh-crop cumin becomes available shortly after harvest, and pricing typically eases as the new crop enters the market compared to late-season, older-stock pricing. If your order timing is flexible, planning around the post-harvest window can meaningfully improve your cost position — ask us for current-season availability when you enquire rather than assuming year-round flat pricing.</p>

<h2>Quality checks worth requesting</h2>
<p>Beyond purity and volatile oil content, ask about admixture screening for other similar-looking seeds, which can be an adulteration risk on cheaper offers in this category generally. A reputable supplier should be able to describe their cleaning and sorting process specifically, not just assert "premium quality" without detail. We can walk you through our sourcing and processing steps for cumin specifically if that level of detail is useful for your own supplier qualification process.</p>
